      "math_academy_id": "q-84994",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation",
      "problem": "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3})$ means the same thing as ${}_{12}{C}_{3}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
